data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**Recommendation:** Keep `mode` as an explicit dependency. Read `emergencyMode` once at the worker's entry point (where the handler starts), then pass it into `alerts`. Don't have `alerts.ts` import config.\n\n**Why:**\n- **Pure and easy to test:** `alerts` stays a plain function of its inputs, so tests don't need to mock modules.\n- **Avoids timing bugs:** worker environment values are often only available per request, not when the module first loads. A direct import can read config too early or keep a stale value.\n- **No hidden coupling:** any caller can see that alert behavior depends on mode.\n- **Pass only what's needed:** use a narrow parameter like `mode: AlertMode` or `{ emergencyMode: boolean }`, not the whole config object. If passing it through many calls gets noisy, use a factory like `createAlerts({ emergencyMode })` instead of an import.\n- **Make it required:** give the parameter no default, so forgetting to pass it fails at compile time.\n\n**Test strategy:**\n- **`alerts` unit tests:** run the same cases with each mode value and assert the visible results: severity, where alerts go, rate limiting and message content. Don't mock `./config`.\n- **`config` unit tests:** check how environment values turn into `emergencyMode`, including defaults, missing values and bad values.\n- **One wiring test at the entry point:** set the environment so emergency mode is on, call the handler and check that alert behavior changes. This catches the main risk of this design, which is reading the config but not passing it through.\n- **Type check in CI:** run `tsc --noEmit` so a missing `mode` argument fails the build."}}

